Now the time engine. Key: resolve local wall time in IANA zone to epoch ms, with gap detection and fold resolution to first occurrence. And format epoch to local parts + offset.

Approach: use Intl.DateTimeFormat with timeZone and formatToParts to get offset for a given instant. Standard technique:

function tzOffsetMs(instant, zone) — compute by formatting instant in zone to get wall-clock parts, then diff against UTC.

function resolveLocal(y,m,d,h,mi, zone):
- candidate = Date.UTC(y,m-1,d,h,mi)
- offset = tzOffsetMs(candidate)  (approximate)
- t1 = candidate - offset
- offset1 = tzOffsetMs(t1); t2 = candidate - offset1
- Check both t1 and t2: format each in zone, see if it equals target local. Collect valid matches.
- If none match → gap (invalid local time).
- If multiple match (fold) → choose the smallest (earliest) instant = first occurrence.
- If one → that's it.

Formatting: need RFC3339 with explicit offset, e.g. 2026-09-24T19:00:00+02:00.

Also need weekday of a local date (date → weekday), which can be computed with Date.UTC(y,m-1,d) and getUTCDay — a calendar date's weekday is timezone-independent. Good.

Also need: given instant, local date string, and offset string.

Let me write the module carefully.

For DateTimeFormat caching with formatToParts.

Note hour cycle: use hourCycle 'h23' to avoid 24:00 issue.

Implementation:

const fmtCache = new Map();
function formatter(zone) {
  let f = fmtCache.get(zone);
  if (!f) {
    f = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', {
      timeZone: zone, hourCycle: 'h23',
      year: 'numeric', month: '2-digit', day: '2-digit',
      hour: '2-digit', minute: '2-digit', second: '2-digit',
    });
    fmtCache.set(zone, f);
  }
  return f;
}

function partsOf(instant, zone) {
  const out = {};
  for (const p of formatter(zone).formatToParts(new Date(instant))) {
    if (p.type !== 'literal') out[p.type] = p.value;
  }
  return { year: +out.year, month: +out.month, day: +out.day, hour: +out.hour, minute: +out.minute, second: +out.second };
}

function offsetMsAt(instant, zone) {
  const p = partsOf(instant, zone);
  const asUTC = Date.UTC(p.year, p.month - 1, p.day, p.hour, p.minute, p.second);
  return asUTC - Math.floor(instant / 1000) * 1000;
}

Hmm, careful: instant may have ms. Use Math.floor(instant/1000)*1000.

resolveLocal(zone, y, mo, d, h, mi):
const want = {year:y, month:mo, day:d, hour:h, minute:mi, second:0};
const naive = Date.UTC(y, mo-1, d, h, mi, 0, 0);
const cands = new Set();
for (const off of [offsetMsAt(naive - 86400000, zone), offsetMsAt(naive, zone), offsetMsAt(naive + 86400000, zone)]) {
  const t = naive - off;
  const p = partsOf(t, zone);
  if (p.year===y && p.month===mo && p.day===d && p.hour===h && p.minute===mi) cands.add(t);
}
if (cands.size === 0) return null; // gap
return Math.min(...cands);

Wait, for a fold: Berlin fall-back 2026-10-25 03:00→02:00, so 02:00-03:00 occurs twice. naive = Date.UTC(2026,9,25,2,30) = 2026-10-25T02:30Z. Offsets near: before transition CEST=+2, after CET=+1. naive-24h → +2; naive → at 02:30Z Berlin time is 03:30 CET = +1; naive+24h → +1. So candidates off in {+2h, +1h}. t for +2h = 00:30Z → Berlin 02:30 CEST ✓. t for +1h = 01:30Z → Berlin 02:30 CET ✓. Both valid → min = 00:30Z = first occurrence ✓. 

Spring forward Berlin 2026-03-29 02:00→03:00: local 02:30 doesn't exist. naive=2026-03-29T02:30Z. offsets: naive-24h → +1 (CET); naive → at 02:30Z Berlin is 04:30 CEST = +2; naive+24h → +2. Candidates: off=+1 → t=01:30Z → Berlin 03:30 CEST ≠ 02:30 ✗. off=+2 → t=00:30Z → Berlin 01:30 CET ✗. cands empty → null ✓. 

Also need reverse: given an instant, the local wall-clock for formatting — partsOf works.

formatLocal(instant, zone) → "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM"
formatOffset(instant, zone) → "+02:00"
formatRfc3339(instant, zone) → "2026-09-24T19:00:00+02:00"

Offset string: off = offsetMsAt(instant, zone); sign; hours = off/3600000; minutes.

Also weekday name from a calendar date (zone independent): use Date.UTC.

Also for availability date validation: parse "YYYY-MM-DD" strictly and check it's a real calendar date.

Also local minutes-of-day for slot grid: computed from the requested wall-clock fields (h*60+mi) and the opens minutes. That avoids offset weirdness: grid is in wall-clock from opening time.

